  <h1>About braininjurycalc.com</h1>

  <p>braininjurycalc.com is an independent, informational site about traumatic brain injury (TBI) compensation. We publish a free calculator for estimating settlement ranges, four in-depth guides on the medical and legal dimensions of TBI, and a methodology page that documents the underlying framework. The site is operated by The Click Lab, a small editorial team focused on catastrophic injury content.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>

  <p>We answer the question a lot of families ask in the weeks after a serious brain injury: <em>what is this case worth, and what should we be doing?</em> The honest answer to that question is "it depends on a lot of factors that need to be worked out by a qualified attorney with the actual medical record in hand" &mdash; but we can describe what those factors are, how settlements get calculated in practice, what the typical ranges look like by severity, and what the practical steps are for protecting the case and the family's long-term interests.</p>

  <p>The calculator is a starting reference for understanding the range. The guides explain the medical classification system (GCS, Rancho, DRS), the damages framework, the life-care planning process, and the common misconceptions about mild TBI, settlement timing, and insurance coverage. The methodology page shows our work.</p>

  <h2>Who we serve</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Patients and families</strong> recently affected by a brain injury who want to understand what to expect from the legal process and what realistic settlement ranges look like.</li>
    <li><strong>Attorneys and paralegals</strong> who use the calculator as a quick sanity check on case valuation, especially in the early intake stage.</li>
    <li><strong>Reporters and writers</strong> who need a citable reference on TBI settlement ranges and the underlying damages framework.</li>
    <li><strong>Researchers and students</strong> studying the intersection of neurotrauma medicine and tort litigation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Editorial principles</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Primary sources.</strong> We cite the Glasgow Coma Scale, the Rancho Los Amigos Cognitive Scale, the Disability Rating Scale, AANS/CNS guidelines, the Brain Injury Association of America (BIAA) clinical materials, peer-reviewed TBI outcome literature, state catastrophic injury statutes, leading appellate opinions, and the published verdict and settlement databases. We do not invent numbers or rely on unsourced claims.</li>
    <li><strong>Plain language.</strong> Specialist terms are defined where first used. Readers should not need a medical degree or a paralegal certification to follow our content.</li>
    <li><strong>Honest about uncertainty.</strong> TBI case valuations vary widely. We do not promise outcomes or predict specific cases. The calculator's range is a starting reference, not a forecast.</li>
    <li><strong>No paid placements.</strong> We do not accept payment to recommend attorneys, law firms, rehabilitation centers, life-care planners, or any other service provider. Our only ad revenue is programmatic Google AdSense.</li>
    <li><strong>Sensitive context.</strong> The reader is often a recently-injured patient, a spouse, or a parent in a difficult moment. We aim for substance without clinicality and information without alarm.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How we are funded</h2>

  <p>The site is supported entirely by programmatic display advertising via Google AdSense. We do not run sponsored content, affiliate links, paid attorney directories, lead generation, or any other indirect monetization. We do not sell or share visitor data with third parties beyond what AdSense and Google Analytics do as standard ad-tech.</p>

  <p>This funding model has implications you should understand: we have no financial incentive to recommend any particular attorney or law firm, because we do not get paid when a reader hires anyone. We have no incentive to inflate or deflate the calculator's outputs, because our revenue does not depend on what the calculator says. Our only commercial interest is that the content be useful enough that people read it and that the ads load alongside it.</p>

  <h2>What we will not do</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>We do not give legal advice.</strong> Nothing on this site is legal advice. We cannot review individual cases, file documents, appear in court, or substitute for a licensed attorney. Catastrophic injury matters require a licensed attorney in your state with neurotrauma experience.</li>
    <li><strong>We do not give medical advice.</strong> Nothing on this site is medical advice. We cannot interpret your imaging, advise on treatment, or substitute for a neurology, rehabilitation, or neuropsychology specialist.</li>
    <li><strong>We do not collect personally identifying information.</strong> The calculator runs entirely in your browser; your inputs are not transmitted to our servers. We do not require accounts, do not request your name or contact information, and do not store details about your injury.</li>
    <li><strong>We do not sell or share visitor data.</strong> Beyond standard analytics and ad-tech (described in detail in our <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a>), we do not share visitor information with anyone, including law firms, marketing companies, or data brokers.</li>
    <li><strong>We do not route readers to specific law firms.</strong> The guides recommend that readers consult a licensed catastrophic injury attorney with neurotrauma experience, but we do not name names, run a directory, or accept referral fees.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who runs the site</h2>

  <p>The Click Lab is a small editorial operation. Our editorial team reviews every piece for technical accuracy, drawing on neurotrauma practice experience and medical documentation and life-care planning expertise. Full bios are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial page</a>.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>

  <p>Editorial questions, citation suggestions, corrections, or interview requests: <a href="mailto:hey@theclicklab.agency">hey@theclicklab.agency</a>. We respond within a few business days. For privacy or legal inquiries about the site itself, the same address works.</p>

